    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 70766,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70766.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 94,
    "turn_text": "All of this takes place inevitably within the political forum because we're now in an election year. The presidential race is going on. And beyond that, it was amazing to me that when the Soleimani strike happened, within minutes, of course, on Twitter, people are sounding off on what they thought happened or what they think happens. That continues to be when the base was attacked. What does that level of noise do to what's happening here?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 70766,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70766.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 95,
    "turn_text": "In some senses, I think it's actually good. If you think about what was happening around the Iraq War, there was this whole rush to basically make everyone that was anti-Iraq War, like the bad person, the other, the Saddam sympathizer. And because communication was being controlled by basically four networks and a couple newspapers, it was pretty easy to do that. Now I think, especially because everything is 24 hours and everyone can communicate with each other for 24 hours, you have people actually having direct discussions about the consequences of war. And we need to be having that. I'd rather have people discussing this, the pros and cons of doing this than just the ignorance that has been occurring for the last 18 years that has basically kept us in these endless wars. I'd rather have people talking about it, having strong debate about it. So that way we don't end up just kind of fighting each other, banning the Dixie chicks, and getting rid of French fries from cafeterias. Remember all that stupid stuff?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Iraq War led to a cultural narrative that labeled anti-war individuals as morally inferior or enemies.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Media consolidation into four major networks and a few newspapers enabled coordinated messaging during the Iraq War era.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The current 24/7 media environment allows for more continuous public discussion on the consequences of war.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Public dialogue about the pros and cons of military intervention is preferable to prolonged ignorance about war impacts.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Endless wars over the past 18 years have largely resulted from sustained public ignorance about their consequences.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Direct public debates about war are necessary to prevent irrational societal reactions like banning the Dixie Chicks or removing French fries from cafeterias.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Public discourse is more trustworthy when it involves diverse voices and real-time interaction.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Historical patterns of censorship and moral panic are likely to recur without active public scrutiny.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The normalization of extreme political reactions (e.g., bans based on symbolic gestures) reflects deeper societal dysfunction caused by lack of informed debate.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "People are capable of engaging in meaningful, rational debate if given access to open and continuous communication channels.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The value of democratic societies lies in citizens' ability to critically evaluate policy decisions through open discourse.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 70766,
